079 #uuid "df003f1c-da11-5c20-b29c-290f8d22c7d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573238 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186572934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573238 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573238 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573238 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186572934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573238 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea10f362-1845-5a00-a8ef-b90f7c8afd1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186572915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186572959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573245 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573245 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573245 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573245 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186573116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573245 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4b7ff8f-af4b-55e7-a7df-45025547835b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573252 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573252 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e0/e0caa2bd7ac6b620ec267d3cc50493701af1f2d6718ddcb090c71f408d2a82ab.png)\n\nThe vectors $\\text{b}$ and $\\text{c}$ are shown in the diagram above. Which of the following could be $-b+c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06291cf0-d6c5-5a7a-a5c7-3922a0916c3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573252 #edb/ref 1074 "We interpret  $-b+c$ as the addition\n\n$$\n(-b)+c,\n$$\n\nwhere $-b$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $b$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $b.$\n\nFinally, we use the triangle law to add the vectors $-b$ and $c:$ \n\n- We can start $-b$ from an arbitrary point on the plane.\n\n- Next, we draw the vector that is equal to $c$ and has its tail placed at the head of $-b.$\n\n- Now, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $-\\text{b}$ to the head of $\\text{c}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/87/873334db27c4cf2d85d262d98050941307026fb7b0341d5c871a5ad8eb565313.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573255 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573255 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573255 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573255 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/71/71434714d4941799da3859979b9a75950994efef09b523da00a6a11523e3b906.png)\n\nGiven that the triangle $△ABC$ shown above is isosceles, the measure of $\\overset{―}{AC}$ must be equal to{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573255 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a6c019b-459a-54b3-94ee-3cfc489e4bfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573255 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AB}$, we have that $\\overset{―}{AC}≅\\overset{―}{CB}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =CB \\\\ 3x-9 & =2x-1 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overset{―}{AC}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =3x-9 \\\\  & =3(8)-9 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573258 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573258 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573258 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/26/26ab73d81c0ec3ef9b2b6fa93de11266fb4d0629b875d7d38636f36926f0f053.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0be768b0-d70e-57fe-9f86-a4cf5ff73d92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573258 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $m∠A=m∠C={45}^{∘}$ and $AB=BC,$ we have an isosceles right triangle. \n\n![](images/e1/e13a301fe37d44657754877ef61b4d5e0e913c309784baa50d53f0d0e5edd913.png)\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573272 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573272 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573272 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}p-2>3 \\\\ 1-2p\\le -7\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573272 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46ac0cd5-cca2-5b10-8e71-7bc6828cb573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573272 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $p$ that satisfy both $p-2>3$ and $1-2p\\le -7$ simultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $p-2>3\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p-2 & >3 \\\\ p-2+2 & >3+2 \\\\ p & >5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/11/1189f49b694afcfef7f10a19b523ebbcce3bb8ab13b69e60ebfd5a6c342ff10e.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve $1-2p\\le -7\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2p & \\le -7 \\\\ 1-2p-1 & \\le -7-1 \\\\ -2p & \\le -8 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2p) & \\ge (-1)\\cdot (-8) \\\\ 2p & \\ge 8 \\\\ \\frac{2p}{2} & \\ge \\frac{8}{2} \\\\ p & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8bf3ae94c8048dcf9060c985300f9a808ae73158eeeef6e0f01f3f922d33ea9.png)\n\nTo find the values of $p$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/1a/1a4cd0c86983a39354eef09ad60d44f3815c082f443a3eb12bd14f6232988f65.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThus, the final solution is $p\\in (5,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573278 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573278 #edb/ref 1069 "q-316642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573278 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573278 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ad/ad5ee5c0c388e0d2a800765288f20350a8332888a54b4eb3fafd5986d6355838.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(-2,-1).$ What is the equation of the line?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573278 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a315de4-d76f-5902-ac75-57586afb2f01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573278 #edb/ref 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,3).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=3.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(-2,-1),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-3}{-2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{-2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=3$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573281 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573281 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573281 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573281 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573281 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{7}{2}u+4w-\\frac{2}{3}u-w=${{field-1}}\n$u\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$w$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573281 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64689025-90b1-5416-8d71-9bc463abeb1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573281 #edb/ref 1074 "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}u+4w-\\frac{2}{3}u-w & = \\\\ (\\frac{7}{2}u-\\frac{2}{3}u)+(4w-w) & = \\\\ (\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{2}{3})u+(4-1)w & = \\\\ (\\frac{21}{6}-\\frac{4}{6})u+3w & = \\\\ (\\frac{21-4}{6})u+3w & = \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}\\,u+3\\,w\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573292 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{3ab}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5b}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90e80b30-af1b-594f-ba5d-4e938ac90b8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573292 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{3ab}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5b} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3ab\\cdot 5b} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{15a{b}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573302 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573302 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573302 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573302 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573302 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573302 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|12z+7|\\ge 5.$\n\n$z\\le$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ or $\\,z$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573302 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf74267c-d6a7-5491-a319-f2e29a265fc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573302 #edb/ref 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $12z+7$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $5.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12z+7 & \\le -5 & \\text{or} &  & 12z+7 & \\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12z+7 & \\le -5 &  &  & 12z+7 & \\ge 5 \\\\ 12z & \\le -12 &  &  & 12z & \\ge -2 \\\\ z & \\le -1 &  &  & z & \\ge -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & \\le -1 & \\text{or} &  & z & \\ge -\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573316 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573316 #edb/ref 1069 "q-25774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573316 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573316 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c1/c15de626535d55df85e756b8c3fffc9c4d50eb5b7e2b049eeec3f2ba6be2f2bb.png)\n\nIn the diagram shown above, $B$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{AD}.$ Find $BC$ if $AD=29\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CD=6.5\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573316 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eaee2e36-f252-5a56-ba54-f7a90c1e698d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573316 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{AD}$, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =\\frac{1}{2}AD \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 29 \\\\  & =14.5\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by the segment addition postulate, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =BC+CD \\\\ 14.5 & =BC+6.5 \\\\ 14.5-6.5 & =BC \\\\ BC & =8\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573324 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573324 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573324 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573324 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $0.9\\,\\text{g}$ expressed in $\\text{mg}?$ Write your answer as a whole number.\n\n$0.9\\,\\text{g}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{mg}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573324 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa1ab2c6-2c4e-5cd2-a497-7648fbd76dfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573324 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\text{kg}$ | $1\\,\\text{kg}=1\\,000\\,\\text{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\text{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\text{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}=1\\,\\text{g}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThere are $1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}$ per $\\text{g},$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1000\\,\\text{mg}}{\\text{g}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.9\\,\\text{g}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.9\\,\\text{g} & =0.9\\,\\text{g}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}}{\\text{g}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\cancel{\\text{g}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}}{\\cancel{\\text{g}}} \\\\  & =0.9\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{mg} \\\\  & =900\\,\\text{mg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.9\\,\\text{g}$ is equivalent to $900\\,\\text{mg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573328 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "049fc9f2-d3fc-5a75-bfdb-fec8013c9477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573328 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573329 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573329 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "082272e6-9cdf-55aa-ad99-e25a5d09e819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573329 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573334 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573334 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f1ab0f5-92dc-5c1f-ba96-99b5b07dfde6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573334 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573344 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573344 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f777a15-5cf7-5fc3-b7be-f8906cc145ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573344 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573347 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573347 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54d1a9ad-b275-5f97-9468-e770c1c82b02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573347 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573359 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac1b35cc-2d6b-5205-826c-79b87f1a771c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573362 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573362 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e693b72e-212d-5f10-9a69-61e7f81e5d6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573362 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573364 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573364 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f3cc3d18-db3e-5f1e-8f24-418da4378855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573364 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573365 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573365 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f87cce5f-9d08-5073-a98a-666fda3fc048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573365 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573367 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573367 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb5fac2d-14b8-5678-8b42-b5dee8d05a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573367 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573255 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
